  "Number": 1,
  "Question": "Which one of the following points is not on the graph of \\(y=\\dfrac{x}{x+1}?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\((0,0)\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\left(-\\dfrac12,-1\\right)\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\left(\\dfrac12,\\dfrac13\\right)\\)",
    "D": "\\((-1,1)\\)",
    "E": "\\((-2,2)\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "A point on this graph must have \\(x+1\\ne0\\), because division by zero is undefined. At \\(x=-1\\), the denominator is zero, so \\((-1,1)\\) cannot lie on the graph.\n\nFor completeness, the other listed inputs give\n\\[\n\\frac0{0+1}=0,\\qquad\n\\frac{-1/2}{1/2}=-1,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{1/2}{3/2}=\\frac13,\\qquad\n\\frac{-2}{-1}=2.\n\\]\nThus A, B, C, and E lie on the graph. The point that does not is \\((-1,1)\\), choice D.",
